[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one bug: the parser reads dates month first. The <CauseCount ticket={4471} /> reports
merged into it describe <CauseCount ticket={4471} of="causes" /> different problems. Each now has its own ticket, and fixing one does not fix the others.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one issue. Closing it when the parser fix ships leaves two bugs open.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The counts above are computed from the export when the page renders, so they follow the data as the desk keeps merging.

## The three problems

**`parser`: dates parse month first.** <CauseCount ticket={4471} cause="parser" /> reports from <CauseCount ticket={4471} cause="parser" of="tenants" />
tenants. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so any
date with a day of 12 or less comes out swapped: 7 April becomes 4 July. This is the bug #4471 names.
Tracked in [issue 1](https://github.com/lmilewicz/mega-what-it-shows/issues/1).

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form tells people to type month first.** <CauseCount ticket={4471} cause="hint" /> reports,
all through the form on the <CauseCount ticket={4471} cause="hint" of="tenants" /> booking-v3 tenants. The form
prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, people follow it, and the form reads their date day first. The symptom looks like the
parser bug, but the parser fix will not touch it. Tracked in
[issue 2](https://github.com/lmilewicz/mega-what-it-shows/issues/2).

**`shift`: the date moves by one day.** <CauseCount ticket={4471} cause="shift" /> reports. Nothing is swapped:
12 May shows as 11 May, a meeting lands on Tuesday in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto, an invite arrives before
the booking. That is a time-zone or daylight-saving conversion. The desk had already marked these as a
one-day move in its notes, but this kb only footnoted them under `parser`, so they never got a ticket.
Tracked in [issue 3](https://github.com/lmilewicz/mega-what-it-shows/issues/3).

## Why they were merged

The desk merges on subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), and all three
problems arrive with the same subjects: "Date is wrong", "Wrong day on the invite". Intake tags did not help either:
the tag is a guess from the reporter's browser, and it disagrees with the tenant's form on dozens of the
reports in the table above. The cause comes from the tenant and the way the report came in, and the desk's own
reading of the body: `src/lib/kb/causes.ts`.

## How the counts were checked

- The export has more rows than reports: its pages overlap, so the same report can appear twice. One id, one report.
- `npm run counts` runs the same `causes.ts` this page runs and prints the reports per cause.

